Chapter: T3 Refusals

 The constructs the design named in advance as expected failures, in one
 program, so the refusal path is exercised rather than assumed. A guard
 that has never fired is worth nothing. Compiling this through the plug
 must FAIL, and the reasons it prints are the measured edge of what the
 Codex IR can carry across a non-binary boundary.

 F1 is the real: IEEE 754 is a binary format by construction, a binary
 significand and a binary exponent, and there is no ternary reading of it.
 F2 is wrapping overflow, which is defined by a binary modulus and has no
 ternary meaning; T3ISA saturates instead, so clamping needs no lowering
 at all while wrapping cannot be expressed. F4 is a band wider than a
 27-trit word.

Section: Body

  f1-real : Real -> Real
  f1-real (x) = x / 2.0

  f2-wrapping : Integer between 0 and 255 wrapping -> Integer between 0 and 255 wrapping
  f2-wrapping (b) = b + 200

  f4-wide : Integer -> Integer
  f4-wide (n) = n * 2

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("f1 " & show (real-to-int (f1-real 9.0)))
    print-line-uni ("f2 " & show (f2-wrapping 100))
    print-line-uni ("f4 " & show (f4-wide 7))
  end
